  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬A∧B$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒¬B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨¬B,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $¬(A∧B).$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬(¬(A∧B)).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒¬B)$ is equivalent to $A∧B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
